{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Take heed to yourself lest you be ensnared after them, after they have been destroyed from before you</b> — after you see that He destroys them from before you, you should set your heart to consider: why were these destroyed? Because of the corrupt deeds in their hands; therefore you too shall not do thus, lest others come and destroy you. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 12:30). And if so, this would constitute a warning and admonition against idol worship, and thus he wrote the expression \"how they worship,\" because Scripture did not punish for idolatry per se but only for slaughtering, burning-offering, libation, and prostration, as it is written (Exodus 22:19) \"none but to YHWH alone\" — things performed toward the heights — and it comes and teaches you here that if the customary mode of worshipping an idolatry involves another action, such as clearing to Pe'or and throwing a stone at Merkulis, which is its prescribed worship, one is liable; and one is liable for slaughtering, burning-offering, libation, and prostration even when not performed in its customary manner — end of his words. But this is not correct, for Scripture says, \"You shall not do thus to YHWH your Elohim\" — if so, it is not a warning against worshipping their elohim, but rather against worshipping the honored Name by means of their mode of worship, as He gives the reason: \"for every abomination of YHWH, which He hates, they did for their elohim,\" etc.\n\nRather, the explanation of the passage is that until now Scripture commanded many times that when we come to the land we shall uproot idolatry and its appurtenances and destroy their name, and it commanded that we do this immediately as soon as we dispossess them. And now it says: when YHWH cuts off those nations from before us and idolatry becomes eradicated from the land and we dwell securely in their land, we might think in our hearts — behold, HaShem has cut off from before us those who worshipped other elohim, because they would bestow full honor through complete worship upon the work of human hands, wood and stone, and it is not fitting to give His honor to another or His praise to idols, for thus He forbade slaughtering, burning-offering, libation, and prostration save to HaShem alone. And the practice among kings is that one who glorifies himself in their honor by setting a crown on his head or donning royal attire is subject to the same law of execution. If so, I too shall do thus for the honored Name, just as the nations did for their elohim, and it will be pleasing before Him. Therefore He warned: \"You shall not do thus\" — for the things that are abominable before Him, they used to do for their elohim. And He did not forbid them because the act itself was honorable and fitting to be performed before HaShem alone, but rather because of the intention that was in them to worship divinity. For \"even their sons and their daughters they would burn in fire to their elohim\" — which is a thing abominable before HaShem, to shed innocent blood, and also to be cruel to the fruit of the womb without mercy. And therefore He said (Deuteronomy 13:1), \"the entire matter that I command you,\" etc. — to warn concerning the sacrificial rites and the Temple service that you shall neither add to it nor subtract from it. And this is truly the purport of this passage.\n\nBut our Sages (Sanhedrin 60b), who derived from here that one who worships idolatry in its customary manner is always liable, even if he clears himself to Pe'or, because Scripture mentioned <b>\"how they worship\"</b>, to teach that in that quality of \"how\" it is called worship and falls under the category of \"and you shall not worship them\" (Exodus 20:5) — but if one throws a stone to Pe'or, or even by way of honor, that one embraces and kisses it, or that one bows and squats before it, he is not liable to death.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, He warned at the beginning of the section (Deuteronomy 12:4), <b>\"You shall not do thus to YHWH your Elohim\"</b>, against worshipping Him like the elohim of the nations upon the high mountains with their offerings, and now in this passage He warned, <b>\"You shall not do thus to YHWH your Elohim\"</b>, against worshipping Him in His Sanctuary by means of their modes of worship."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Ibn Ezra (referred to as איכה יעבדו) by his characteristic phrase; the abbreviation עכ\"ל ('end of his words') closes the cited view. The passage beginning 'והנהוג במלכים' is Ramban's paraphrase of the mistaken reasoning he attributes to the hypothetical idolater."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:49:40.661150317Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}